WEBVTT

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.770
But the only myosin heads that can bind to those newly exposed sites are ones that are ready for action.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:08.770
That is, the ones that have already grabbed a molecule of ATP that’s been floating around,

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:11.710
and broken it down into ADP and the leftover phosphate.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.370
When a myosin head does that, it moves into an extended position, kinda like a stretched

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:21.030
spring -- still holding on to the ADP and phosphate, and still storing the energy that

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.750
was released when they were broken apart.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.970
So after all that, with the myosin primed for action and the bodyguards out of the way,

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:30.250
the myosin finally binds to actin, and it is beautiful.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.250
When they bind, the myosin releases all that stored energy, and -- in the excitement of

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:39.110
it all -- the myosin changes shape. It pulls on its precious actin strand, kind of like

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:40.570
pulling a rope hand over fist.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.630
In the process, it shrinks the whole sarcomere, and contracts the muscle.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.650
That’s the sliding part of the sliding filament model.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.710
Now, with its energy spent, that little head has no use for the ADP and the phosphate.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.930
So they un-bind with it, because -- remember Rule Number Two, changing shape encourages

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.710
proteins to bind or unbind with stuff. That unbinding causes a small change in its shape,

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.050
which lets a fresh ATP binds there in its place.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:07.890
That binding causes another shape change. But this time, it causes the myosin to release

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:13.490
from the actin, in a tear-jerking scene like some microscopic re-creation of the finale from Titanic.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.610
But fear not! This epic is not quite over!

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.310
Because this is when the myosin breaks down its new molecule of ATP into ADP and a phosphate,

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:25.830
which moves it into the armed position yet again, getting it ready for its next rendezvous.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:30.450
And meanwhile, those calcium pumps are working hard to restock the calcium in the sarcoplasmic

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.570
reticulum. So they start grabbing the calcium that’s floating around, causing calcium to unbind from the

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:40.270
troponin. When it unbinds, the resulting shape-change puts the tropomyosin bodyguards back into place.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:40.990
It’s a circle.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.650
Or potentially a big Hollywood franchise. With lots and lots of sequels.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.230
It keeps repeating itself many, many times every moment, while I sit here and talk, and

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:56.450
while you sit there and eat and text and take notes, the whole drama replaying itself over and over.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.630
Kind of like you’ll have to play this video over and over again to get all the little

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.770
steps of the sliding filament model straightened out.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:05.130
But hey, some stories get better the more you hear them.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:09.650
If you do watch this one again, you will re-learn that your smooth, cardiac, and skeletal muscles

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:14.390
create movement by contracting and releasing in a process called the sliding filament model.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.510
You’d also re-learn that your skeletal muscles are constructed like a rope made of bundles

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:23.650
of protein fibers, and that the smallest strands are your actin and myosin myofilaments. Its

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:28.230
their use of calcium and ATP that causes the binding and unbinding that makes sarcomeres

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.630
contract and relax.
